{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-07): REFUSE, with an allowed force override.\n\nOn detecting a secret in an event body at the append_event seam, the write is REFUSED — the event does not land. A force override is available for the operator judgment call (same escape-hatch shape as --force-close), so a false positive can never permanently block a legitimate write.\n\nThis settles the refuse-vs-redact question that was blocking implementation. Redaction is NOT adopted: silently rewriting a caller's payload makes the stored event differ from what the caller believes it wrote, and in an event-sourced store that divergence is unrecoverable after the fact. Refusing is loud, reversible, and leaves the caller in control.\n\nImplementation notes implied by the decision:\n- refusal must name WHICH detector fired and where, so the override is an informed choice rather than a blind retry;\n- the refusal path must not itself echo the secret into stderr or logs;\n- the override must be recorded on the event (who forced, why) so a forced write is auditable rather than indistinguishable from a clean one.\n\nThis also discharges the dependency noted on 2a76 AC5, which was marked [operator-attested] pending exactly this ruling."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786096903310378001,"uuid":"f1bc5307-a3d5-44df-af02-6c1468241b09"}